  "body": "The Storage API presently returns a string representation of the values that were put into it, thus rendering all values to be truthy. For e.g.\r\n\r\n```\r\nlocalStorage.setItem(\"key\", false);\r\n\r\nlocalStorage.getItem(\"key\") // returns the string \"false\" which is a truthy value\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe behavior is different for non-existing keys. For keys that do not exist, falsey values are returned. For e.g.\r\n\r\n`localStorage.getItem(\"doesNotExist\"); // returns null, which is a falsey value`\r\n\r\nThese two behaviors are described in the two following videos:\r\n\r\na) JavaScript: A request to change the HTML5 Storage API\r\nhttps://youtu.be/tF7kqpsy884\r\n\r\nb) JavaScript: Beware: the Storage API functions always return truthy values\r\nhttps://youtu.be/gcW2HwRL9xo\r\n\r\n**Proposal**\r\nCould you please make this API consistent across all keys, whether existing in the storage or not, so that they return falsey values where they should?\r\n\r\n**Backward Compatibility With Existing Storage API**\r\nThis would not be a breaking change as any code that presently uses the Storage API would only have extra checks that cast the truthy string values returned by the Storage API into falsey values before processing it.\r\n\r\nAnd yet others will have written code so as to not put any falsey values at all in Storage.",
